= Summoning Mechanics

Two resources are gained upon defeating an enemy, a *corpse* and *soul juice* (name not final).
Corpses are dropped at the ground where a unit dies, whereas soul juice is a universal resource shared by all player units (similar to gold).
Soul juice is the more premium currency.
A large group of weak enemies may drop many corpses but very little soul juice, whereas a single very strong enemy may drop a lot of soul juice but only one corpse.
Soul juice has a cap of 100; weak enemies probably drop like 3 soul juice whereas bosses can drop up to 50 soul juice.

= Necromancers

Most of the necromancers are weak caster combat units who serve as support, summoner, or utility units.

== Plague guy

Specialty is summoning zombies.
Zombies are corpse-expensive require litte soul juice.
His auto attack infects enemies with plague, which does a small amount of damage over time and reanimates the unit as a zombie when it falls.
Can spend soul juice to imbue zombies with plague or blow them up for area damage.

== Skeleton friend

Summons skeletons and curses the enemy with debuffs.
Skeletons require a corpse and a moderate amount of soul juice.
Skeletons can wield a variety of "slayer" type weapons.
Basically the idea behind skeleton friend is to be able to pick counters to specific strong enemies.

== Ghost lady

Summons ghosts, which require no corpse but a high amount of soul juice.
Ghosts can come in many different classes but have the ability to become intangible and pass through walls maybe?
She can spend soul juice to warp allied units maybe?
The idea behind ghost lady is that she focuses on army mobility.


